docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:119:   > **8. How much does weight loss surgery cost in Perth?**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:120:   > Costs depend on the pathway, procedure, hospital, private health cover, waiting periods, hospital excess, anaesthetist and other fees. Any applicable estimate or range should be discussed at consultation after your team understands your situation. Confirm any Medicare rebate or private-cover contribution for your circumstances.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:122:   > **9. What is the 30/30 eating rule after surgery?**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:123:   > The 30/30 rule is an eating habit often recommended after bariatric surgery: take around 30 minutes to eat each meal, and avoid drinking fluids for 30 minutes either side of eating. Your dietitian will confirm the exact protocol the practice recommends for you. [30/30 protocol to be confirmed with the practice before publication]
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:125:   > **10. Who may not meet the Medicare pathway for weight loss surgery?**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:128:   > **11. Does Medicare cover weight loss surgery in Australia?**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:129:   > Medicare may provide rebates for eligible specialist consultations and MBS-listed bariatric surgery services when current criteria are met. The relevant procedure items are 31575 for sleeve gastrectomy, 31572 for Roux-en-Y gastric bypass and 31581 for BPD/duodenal switch. Private health cover, waiting periods, hospital excess and anaesthetist fees can affect out-of-pocket costs, so confirm what applies before deciding.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:131:   > **12. What are the 10-year outcomes after weight loss surgery?**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:134:   Verify the clinic's exact 30/30 protocol and the current MBS wording before publication. These answers contain no dollar figures; any cost range belongs in the consultation discussion.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:178:> A BMI of 27 or above, particularly where a weight-related health condition is present, is a reason to request an individual medical assessment for non-surgical options. Surgical Medicare eligibility must be assessed separately; current MBS criteria refer to BMI 40+, or BMI 35–39.9 with a major co-morbidity, with suitability assessed by your surgeon.
