docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:251:> Medicare rebates apply to most bariatric procedures when eligibility criteria are met, and private health insurance with a gold-tier or equivalent policy covers the hospital and theatre component. The combination of both typically means a significant portion of the total cost is covered. Out-of-pocket costs vary depending on your level of cover, excess, and anaesthetist fees. Our team provides a clear, itemised cost breakdown before any commitment is made, so you know exactly where you stand.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:253:> Medicare benefits and private-health benefits may apply when the relevant MBS, clinical, referral and policy requirements are met. Coverage, waiting periods, exclusions, excesses and out-of-pocket costs vary, so ask Medicare and your insurer to confirm what applies and discuss an itemised estimate at consultation.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:259:The GLP-1 occurrences in the medical-weight-management procedure copy (source lines 209-214) and FAQ copy (source lines 303-310) are already handled by Must-fix 1 and Must-fix 2 above; do not add a second replacement here. **OPEN — pending TGA/legal decision:** if medication wording is retained after sign-off, use active-ingredient/class names only (never brands).
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:303:### G3 — concrete cut-list, FAQ answers and verified internal links
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:322:  > 1. **Initial enquiry and GP referral:** most patients start with a GP referral, which can help with Medicare and private health requirements; if you contact OSWA directly, the team can guide you on what is needed. 2. **Consultation with your surgeon:** your surgeon reviews your history, goals and health conditions, then explains which surgical or non-surgical pathways may be appropriate. 3. **Wider team assessment:** this may include physician, dietitian and exercise physiology input. 4. **Pre-surgery preparation:** you receive procedure-specific preparation advice, including any pre-operative diet. 5. **Procedure day:** surgery is usually laparoscopic, with hospital stay and recovery guidance tailored to the procedure. 6. **Long-term aftercare:** follow-up, nutrition monitoring and habit support continue after surgery.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:371:- **Replace the seven-answer FAQ block** (source lines 270-342):
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:373:  > ### FAQs (7 questions)
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:377:  > **2. Is bariatric surgery available on Medicare or private health insurance?** Medicare rebates apply to most bariatric procedures when eligibility criteria are met, and private health insurance with a gold-tier or equivalent policy covers the hospital and theatre component. The combination of both typically means a significant portion of the total cost is covered. Out-of-pocket costs vary depending on your level of cover, excess, and anaesthetist fees. Our team provides a clear, itemised cost breakdown before any commitment is made, so you know exactly where you stand.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:389:  Replace it with the canonical 11-question block in the “Paste-ready FAQ replacement” section below.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:393:> Obesity Surgery WA offers [gastric sleeve surgery](/gastric-sleeve-perth), [gastric bypass surgery](/gastric-bypass-perth), [mini gastric bypass](/mini-gastric-bypass), [gastric band surgery](/gastric-band), [revision bariatric surgery](/revision-bariatric-surgery) and [non-surgical weight management](/non-surgical-weight-loss). See [eligibility](/am-i-eligible), [Medicare and private health cover](/medicare-private-health-cover), [GP referral](/gp-referral), [surgeons](/surgeons), [FAQs](/faqs) and [book a consultation](/book-consultation).
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:397:#### Paste-ready FAQ replacement
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:399:Replace the existing FAQ heading and answers (source lines 270-342) with:
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:401:> ### FAQs
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:403:> **1. How much does bariatric surgery cost in Perth?**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:407:> **2. Is bariatric surgery covered by Medicare or private health insurance?**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:409:> Medicare benefits may apply when the relevant MBS item, clinical and referral requirements are met, but eligibility is assessed individually. Verify the planned item number: 31575 is sleeve gastrectomy, 31572 is Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, and 31581 is BPD/duodenal switch. Private-health benefits depend on the fund, policy, waiting periods and exclusions.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:423:> **6. What is the 30/30 eating rule after bariatric surgery?**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:425:> The 30/30 rule is an eating habit often recommended after bariatric surgery: take around 30 minutes to eat each meal, and avoid drinking fluids for 30 minutes either side of eating. Your dietitian will confirm the exact protocol the practice recommends for you. [30/30 protocol to be confirmed with the practice before publication]
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:433:> Aftercare can include surgeon reviews, blood tests, dietitian support, supplements where prescribed and help adapting eating. Over 10 years, experiences vary: some people maintain changes, some regain weight, develop nutritional issues or need further treatment. No single 10-year result can be promised, so ongoing follow-up remains important.
