content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:15:> Medicare rebates may apply when MBS criteria are met. For Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, the relevant MBS item is 31572; your surgeon will confirm the appropriate Medicare item number, rebate eligibility and any private health fund requirements before surgery. Private health insurance with appropriate hospital cover may contribute to hospital and theatre costs, but out-of-pocket costs vary by fund and policy.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:53:> Gastric bypass surgery is one of the oldest, most well-known bariatric procedures in Australia, with the Roux-en-Y gastric bypass being the most common and often the preferred option for those managing associated health conditions such as type 2 diabetes or gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD) in addition to overweight or obesity.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:75:> Improvement in other conditions linked to overweight and obesity, such as high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, and joint pain.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:101:7. **Residual coverage and candidate wording remains in the eligibility and FAQ copy**
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:105:> Factors such as type 2 diabetes or significant acid reflux may be discussed during assessment, but they do not establish suitability or Medicare eligibility. Your surgeon will assess your health, anatomy, treatment history and ability to follow long-term dietary and supplement requirements.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:107:> Loose skin is common after significant weight loss. How much loose skin your weight loss results in depends on your age, your genetics, your skin elasticity, and of course, how much weight you lose. Exercise, particularly resistance training, can help with body composition during your weight loss phase. You might look into plastic surgery for loose skin (‘body contouring’ and ‘skin removal’) as an option down the track, though bear in mind it is not covered by the same funding pathways.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:111:> A referral isn't required to make an enquiry or book a consultation, but having one is worthwhile. It allows Medicare rebates to apply to your specialist consultation fee, reducing your out-of-pocket cost for the appointment. It also ensures your surgeon has your relevant medical history from the outset. If you don't yet have a referral, our team can advise you on next steps.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:113:> A referral is not required to make an enquiry or book a consultation. A valid referral may allow a Medicare rebate for an eligible specialist consultation, subject to Medicare rules; it does not guarantee a rebate or cover the full fee. It can also help your surgeon receive relevant medical history, and the team can advise you if you do not yet have one.
