    53	## Section guidance
    54	
    55	### Hero
    56	- Headline direction: Frame the page as covering more than just surgery — it is about finding the right starting point. The headline should signal expertise and approachability in the same breath.
    57	- Subheadline: 2–3 sentences. Acknowledge the emotional reality of years of trying (without framing the reader as a failure). Introduce OSWA as a team — surgeons, physicians, and dietitians — not just a surgical service. Make clear the team meets the reader where they are, including if they are not yet ready for surgery.
    58	
    59	### Stats bar (4 stats)
    60	Use these factual anchors. Keep the BMI stat explicitly assessment-only; it is not a surgical MBS threshold:
    61	
    62	| Label | Value | Note |
    63	|---|---|---|
    64	| Dietitians on the team | APD | Accredited Practising Dietitians; ongoing support is part of the plan, not an afterthought |
    65	| Perth clinics | 2 | Booragoon and Mandurah |
    66	| Decades of experience | 20+ | Bariatric surgery in Perth |
    67	| Medical-team assessment from | BMI 27+ | Individual assessment; not a surgical MBS threshold |
    68	
    69	### What is weight loss surgery — and why it is only part of the picture
    70	- Section title: "What we mean by weight loss surgery, and why it is only part of the picture"
    71	- ~4 paragraphs (this page warrants a slightly longer treatment given its breadth)
    72	- Para 1: Define weight loss surgery / bariatric surgery in plain terms — procedures that change stomach size or function to help the patient eat less and feel full sooner. Name the most common procedures: gastric sleeve, gastric bypass, adjustable gastric band. State that these procedures have been used in Australia for many years, while likely benefits, risks and suitability vary by person.
    73	- Para 2: Establish clearly that surgery is not the only road, and not always the first. Explain that obesity is a medical condition, not a willpower failure — this framing matters because it shapes what good treatment looks like. The right answer might be medication, a structured very low calorie diet, dietitian support, an endoscopic option, or surgery — often in combination over time.
    74	- Para 3: Introduce the team composition — not just surgeons; also physicians who manage the medical side of weight, and specialist dietitians whose support is central to the care plan. Whoever the patient sees first, they are stepping into a team that can move them along the spectrum of care as their needs change.
    75	- Para 4: State the aim of the page plainly — to help the reader understand the full range of options so they can make an informed decision, rather than feeling pushed toward any single one.
    76	- Closing comparison note (below paragraphs): Give the reader a quick mental model of the spectrum: medical management (GLP-1 medication, very low calorie diet, dietitian support) at one end, surgical procedures (sleeve, bypass, band) at the other. Acknowledge that many people move between them over time, and there is no single correct starting point.
    77	
    78	### Eligibility section
    79	- BMI summary to anchor the section: "You do not need a very high BMI to start a conversation. A BMI of 27 or above, particularly where a weight-related health condition is present, is a reason to request an individual medical assessment for non-surgical options. For the Medicare-listed surgical pathway, current MBS thresholds refer to BMI 40+, or BMI 35–39.9 with a major co-morbidity. We will help you understand what needs to be assessed."
    80	- Write each of the following points — keep them accessible and non-clinical:
    81	  - BMI around 27+ with a condition such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea: invite an individual medical assessment for non-surgical options
    82	  - Current MBS surgical thresholds: BMI 40+, or BMI 35–39.9 with a major co-morbidity; clinical suitability is assessed individually
    83	  - Higher BMI with prior attempts at weight loss that did not hold: surgical and non-surgical options can be assessed together
    84	  - Readiness to engage with ongoing support (dietitian appointments) as part of any pathway
    85	  - Age, general health, previous surgery, and current medications are all considered at assessment
    86	  - If the reader is unsure what can be assessed: that uncertainty is exactly what an initial consultation is for — explain the options without promising eligibility
    87	
    88	### How it works (5 steps)
    89	Keep steps in this order — 1–2 sentences covering the content described:
    90	
    91	1. **An honest first consultation** — the team listens to history, prior attempts, health, and what the patient actually wants; no judgement; this conversation shapes everything that follows
    92	2. **Assessment by the right team member** — depending on the situation, this may be a physician, surgeon, or dietitian first; patients are matched to the part of the team that fits where they are starting from
    93	3. **A pathway proposed, not prescribed** — the team lays out realistic options from medical management through to surgery, explains likely benefits and trade-offs of each; the patient decides together with the team
    94	4. **Treatment, with support built in** — whether the plan involves medication, a very low calorie diet, an endoscopic option, or an operation, the dietitians and clinical team stay involved throughout
    95	5. **Long-term follow-up** — weight management is ongoing; the team reviews progress, adjusts the plan as needs change, and provides follow-up over time
    96	
    97	### Benefits section
    98	- Section title: "How a team-based approach supports care"
    99	- 6 bullets, one idea each:
   100	  1. Assessment across the full spectrum of care means the patient is not limited to a single treatment
   101	  2. Dedicated dietitians mean nutritional support is consistent and central to the care plan and long-term follow-up
   102	  3. Physicians can manage related conditions such as type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure alongside weight
   103	  4. If the patient starts with medical management and later decides surgery is right, the transition happens within the same team that already knows their history
   104	  5. Both Perth clinic locations (Booragoon and Mandurah) keep appointments and aftercare close to home
   105	  6. Many people experience improvements in weight-related conditions and day-to-day quality of life — individual results vary and no specific outcome can be promised
   106	
   107	### Risks — honest section
   108	- Section title: "Being honest about the trade-offs"
   109	- Intro paragraph: Acknowledge that every option on the spectrum carries its own considerations. Frame this as a genuine service to the reader — the team will not pretend otherwise. Medications have side effects. Very low calorie diets need supervision. Surgery is a significant step with real risks and recovery. The clinician should explain how these considerations relate to the reader's medical situation before any commitment.
   110	- Risk bullets to address (one sentence each):
   111	  - GLP-1 medications can cause nausea, digestive symptoms and other side effects; not suitable for everyone
   112	  - Very low calorie diets require supervision and are not designed as a permanent eating pattern
   113	  - Surgical procedures carry risks including bleeding, infection, leaks, blood clots and anaesthetic reactions
   114	  - After surgery, lifelong changes to eating and, in some cases, vitamin supplementation are needed
   115	  - Outcomes vary, and weight regain can occur; ongoing follow-up remains part of care
   116	  - The best option for someone else may not be right for this reader — individual assessment matters
   117	
   118	### Cost paragraph
   119	Cover: cost depends entirely on which pathway is chosen — medical management and dietitian support look very different in cost to surgery; within surgery the procedure type matters too; private health cover, Medicare rebates, and in some cases superannuation access can all play a part; any applicable estimate or range should be discussed at consultation for the specific situation. Do not quote specific dollar figures.
   120	
   121	### Conversion CTA headline
   122	Direction: The reader on this page is at an earlier stage than on the procedure-specific pages — they are still working out where to start. The CTA headline should validate that uncertainty as the right reason to reach out, not an obstacle.
   123	Reference tone: "Not sure where to start? That is exactly the right reason to talk to us."
