content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-85-- ~3 paragraphs
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-86-- **Para 1:** Define the Roux-en-Y procedure in plain terms. The surgeon creates a small stomach pouch (roughly the size of an egg) and connects it directly to a lower section of the small intestine. Food travels a shorter path, so the patient feels full sooner and the body absorbs fewer kilojoules.
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-87-- **Para 2:** Explain the dual mechanism — volume restriction (smaller pouch) and hormonal change (gut hormones controlling hunger, fullness and blood sugar are altered by the rerouting). This contributes to the evidence base for type 2 diabetes management. Some patients may see blood sugar changes early after surgery, but the extent, timing and duration of improvement vary between individuals.
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md:88:- **Para 3:** Note that the operation may be performed keyhole (laparoscopic) through small incisions. Wound size, pain, scarring, recovery time and complications vary; the surgeon confirms whether a keyhole approach is appropriate at consultation.
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-89-
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-90-**Comparison note to weave in:** Compared with a gastric sleeve, gastric bypass may be discussed for some patients with type 2 diabetes or significant reflux, but it is a more involved operation with lifelong vitamin needs. The right choice is made with the surgeon based on the individual's health, goals and risk profile.
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-91-
--
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-107-1. **GP referral** — The reader asks their GP for a referral to the practice. A referral may allow a Medicare rebate for an eligible consultation, subject to Medicare rules, and is the first formal step toward seeing a surgeon.
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-108-2. **Orientation consultation** — The reader meets their surgeon to discuss health, goals and whether a bypass is the right fit. No pressure to decide on the day. Frame this as an open conversation, not a sales meeting.
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-109-3. **Pre-operative assessment** — The patient sees dietitians and physicians to prepare for surgery and plan nutrition. A pre-surgery eating plan may be prescribed to reduce liver size; its purpose, duration and risks are explained individually. This is team-led support, not a handout.
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md:110:4. **Surgery** — Performed under general anaesthetic using a keyhole approach where appropriate, at St John of God Murdoch or Subiaco (hospital identity to be verified — open question). Keep language clinical and calm; timing varies by patient.
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md:111:5. **Hospital recovery** — The care team manages comfort, starts the patient on fluids, and monitors movement and recovery. Length of stay and recovery timing vary with clinical needs and are confirmed individually.
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-112-6. **Ongoing support** — Regular follow-up with surgeon and dietitian well beyond the operation. Position this as a long-term relationship, not a transaction. If weight loss stalls, the team reviews and adjusts rather than leaving the patient on their own.
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-113-
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-114----
--
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-128-- **Intro paragraph tone:** Acknowledge directly that the bypass is a major operation and no surgery is without risk. Give the patient the full picture before deciding, including serious complications that can occur, and explain that the surgeon will discuss how each risk applies individually.
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-129-- **Risk bullets to address:**
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-130-  1. General surgical risks — bleeding, infection, blood clots, reaction to anaesthetic.
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md:131:  2. Risk of a leak at one of the new surgical connections — monitored closely during the hospital stay.
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-132-  3. Lifelong need for vitamin and mineral supplements, because the rerouting reduces nutrient absorption.
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-133-  4. Dumping syndrome — feeling unwell after eating sugary or fatty foods; symptoms may change as eating habits adjust. Your dietitian will explain what to watch for and when to seek advice.
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-134-
--
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-170----
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-171-
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-172-## Tone and compliance notes
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md:173:- **Hospitals (OPEN):** hospital identity is unverified — briefs and drafts name different facilities. Treat every hospital name in public copy as [hospital identity to be verified] until the client confirms.
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-174-- **Medical advertising:** No guarantees. Use phrases like "clinical averages", "results vary", "individual results depend on", "outcomes vary between individuals", "your surgeon will advise", "after discussion with their doctor".
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-175-- **AHPRA/TGA:** No testimonials, no before/after claims, no guaranteed outcomes. The diabetes claim ("some patients reduce medication") must be qualified with "after discussion with their doctor" and "outcomes vary between individuals".
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-176-- **Voice:** Plain-language, empathetic, direct. Slightly more clinical than the sleeve page is appropriate given the procedure's complexity — but still written for a nervous patient doing research, not for a medical journal.
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-177-- **Reader:** A Perth adult, likely with type 2 diabetes or significant reflux, who has been researching weight loss surgery and is now comparing procedures. They want to know whether bypass is right for them specifically, and they are sceptical of hype.
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md:178:- **Surgeon credentials to include:** Dr Harsha Chandraratna (MBBS, FRACS, Bariatric Surgeon; personal bariatric surgery experience; lecturer at University of Notre Dame) and Dr Stephen Watson (MBBS, FRACS, Bariatric Surgeon; keyhole bypass and other weight-loss procedures at SJOG Murdoch and Subiaco). Both operate at St John of God Murdoch and Subiaco (hospital identity to be verified — open question).
content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md-179-- **Clinic locations to mention:** Consultations at Booragoon (Garden City Specialist Centre) and Mandurah (WA Cardiology Centre).
--
