     1	# Content Brief: Gastric Bypass Surgery Perth
     2	**URL:** /gastric-bypass-perth
     3	**Primary keyword:** gastric bypass surgery perth
     4	**Search intent:** commercial / informational
     5	**Target word count:** 1,200–1,500 words (matches brief; top SERP competitors are clinic procedure pages running ~1,000–1,800w — depth on bypass-vs-sleeve, cost transparency, and diabetes outcomes is the differentiator, not raw length)
     6	
     7	## Page purpose
     8	This page serves patients researching Roux-en-Y gastric bypass in Perth — often people with type 2 diabetes or significant reflux for whom bypass may be discussed as one option among others. It must explain the procedure clearly, address "bypass vs sleeve" comparisons honestly without implying a universal advantage, and move the reader toward a consultation without applying pressure.
     9	
    10	---
    11	
    12	## Keyword Intelligence
    13	
    14	### Primary Keyword
    15	- **Keyword:** gastric bypass surgery perth
    16	- **AU Monthly Volume:** 200
    17	- **Keyword Difficulty:** 42/100
    18	- **CPC (AU):** ~$1
    19	
    20	### Secondary Keywords (target naturally in body copy)
    21	| Keyword | AU Vol/mo | KD |
    22	|---|---|---|
    23	| gastric bypass perth | 200 | 42 |
    24	| mini gastric bypass perth | 100 | 0 |
    25	| roux en y gastric bypass | 450 | 11 |
    26	| gastric sleeve vs bypass | 350 | 2 |
    27	| gastric bypass surgery cost australia | 150 | 0 |
    28	| gastric bypass surgery recovery time | 100 | 22 |
    29	| what can you never eat again after gastric bypass | 250 | 1 |
    30	| weight loss surgery perth | 100 | 2 |
    31	
    32	### Semantic Terms to Include
    33	These are not keyword-stuff targets — they are terms that signal topical depth to Google. Weave them naturally into the copy:
    34	
    35	Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, bariatric surgery, laparoscopic / keyhole surgery, stomach pouch, small intestine rerouting, type 2 diabetes and blood sugar control, gut hormones, ghrelin, excess weight loss, acid reflux / GORD, dumping syndrome, vitamin and mineral supplements, BMI eligibility, Medicare rebate, private health cover, St John of God Murdoch, anastomotic leak, general anaesthetic, dietitian follow-up.
    36	
    37	### SERP Landscape (AU)
    38	The SERP is dominated by Perth bariatric clinic and surgeon procedure pages (perthweightlosssurgery.com.au, aurorabariatrics, perthsurgicalbariatrics, obesitysurgerywa, evolvebariatrics, drkevindolan) plus one high-authority hospital page (St John of God Murdoch, DR 70). Most ranking domains have low DR (7–26), so the niche is winnable on relevance and depth rather than backlink strength. Intent is mixed commercial/informational: searchers want to understand the procedure AND find a Perth surgeon to book. Google also surfaces a People Also Ask block (cost, the 30-30 rule, Medicare coverage, minimum weight) and dedicated Roux-en-Y bypass pages (evolvebariatrics, drkevindolan) — signalling that procedure-specific RYGB pages, not generic bariatric hubs, are what rank for this exact query.
    39	
    40	Top 5 ranking pages:
    41	| # | Domain | Page Title |
    42	|---|---|---|
    43	| 1 | advancesurgical.com.au | Advance Surgical \| Bariatric Surgeon Perth \| Weight Loss |
    44	| 2 | perthweightlosssurgery.com.au | Bariatric Surgeon Dr Siva Gounder: Weight Loss Surgery Perth |
    45	| 3 | aurorabariatrics.com.au | Perth Bariatric Surgery \| Dr Ruth Blackham \| Aurora |
    46	| 4 | sjog.org.au | Bariatric surgery \| St John of God Murdoch Hospital |
    47	| 5 | perthsurgicalbariatrics.com.au | Perth Surgical & Bariatrics \| Top-Rated Weight Loss Surgeons |
    48	
    49	### Content Gap Opportunity
    50	Clear opportunities for OSWA to own:
    51	1. **Cost transparency** — PAA shows explicit demand for "How much does gastric bypass cost in Perth?" and "Can gastric bypass be covered by Medicare?" — most competitor pages dodge cost. The brief's transparent itemised-estimate plus measured explanation of how Medicare and private health cover may apply directly fills this gap.
    52	2. **Bypass vs sleeve comparison** — "gastric sleeve vs bypass" / "gastric bypass vs sleeve" carry strong AU volume (350+250) at near-zero difficulty, yet competitor bypass pages rarely tackle the comparison honestly. The brief's bypass-vs-sleeve note should explain procedure-specific trade-offs, including potential diabetes or reflux considerations, without implying a universal advantage.
    53	3. **Type 2 diabetes outcomes** — No top result leads with type 2 diabetes outcomes despite this being a clinically relevant evidence-based angle for the bypass. Owning the "bypass for type 2 diabetes in Perth" angle is open, provided all outcomes are qualified and individualised.
    54	4. **Plain-language clinical clarity + named surgeons** — AHPRA rules block competitors from using before/after and testimonials, so the page must win on plain-language clinical clarity and named, credentialled surgeons (Dr Chandraratna, Dr Watson) operating at the same DR-70 SJOG Murdoch facility that already ranks.
    55	5. **Long-tail FAQ capture** — Recovery-time and "what you can never eat again" queries have meaningful volume and low KD — weave into FAQ for long-tail capture.
    56	
    57	### Recommended Word Count
    58	1,200–1,500 words (matches brief; top SERP competitors are clinic procedure pages running ~1,000–1,800w — depth on bypass-vs-sleeve, cost transparency, and diabetes outcomes is the differentiator, not raw length).
    59	
    60	---
    61	
    62	## Section guidance
    63	
    64	### Hero
    65	- **Headline direction:** Name the procedure and location: "Gastric bypass surgery in Perth." A secondary phrase about candour or clarity can follow (the existing direction: "explained without the pressure").
    66	- **Subheadline:** 2–3 sentences. Validate the reader's experience of repeated failed dieting. Introduce the Roux-en-Y gastric bypass as a well-studied weight-loss procedure. Note that it may be discussed when type 2 diabetes is part of the picture, while making clear that procedure choice depends on individual assessment.
    67	
    68	---
    69	
    70	### Stats bar (4 stats)
    71	Keep these figures verbatim as strategic brief guidance; do not paste them into publishable copy unless the verification note below is satisfied.
    72	
    73	| Label | Value | Note |
    74	|---|---|---|
    75	| Expected weight loss | 70–80% | Excess weight, clinical average. Results vary between individuals. |
    76	| Time in surgery | ~120 min | Performed under general anaesthetic, keyhole technique. |
    77	| Hospital stay | 2–3 nights | At St John of God Murdoch or Subiaco (hospital identity to be verified — open question). |
    78	| Return to most activity | 3–4 weeks | Light duties often sooner, heavy lifting later. |
    79	
    80	These four figures are strategic brief guidance, not paste-ready copy. Retain the 70–80% claim and the operation-time, hospital-stay and recovery-timing stats only with the note: **verify: requires a cited clinical source + practice sign-off before publication**.
    81	
    82	---
    83	
    84	### What is a gastric bypass?
    85	- ~3 paragraphs
    86	- **Para 1:** Define the Roux-en-Y procedure in plain terms. The surgeon creates a small stomach pouch (roughly the size of an egg) and connects it directly to a lower section of the small intestine. Food travels a shorter path, so the patient feels full sooner and the body absorbs fewer kilojoules.
    87	- **Para 2:** Explain the dual mechanism — volume restriction (smaller pouch) and hormonal change (gut hormones controlling hunger, fullness and blood sugar are altered by the rerouting). This contributes to the evidence base for type 2 diabetes management. Some patients may see blood sugar changes early after surgery, but the extent, timing and duration of improvement vary between individuals.
    88	- **Para 3:** Note that the operation may be performed keyhole (laparoscopic) through small incisions. Wound size, pain, scarring, recovery time and complications vary; the surgeon confirms whether a keyhole approach is appropriate at consultation.
    89	
    90	**Comparison note to weave in:** Compared with a gastric sleeve, gastric bypass may be discussed for some patients with type 2 diabetes or significant reflux, but it is a more involved operation with lifelong vitamin needs. The right choice is made with the surgeon based on the individual's health, goals and risk profile.
    91	
    92	---
    93	
    94	### Eligibility
    95	- **BMI note:** Eligibility is assessed individually. BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with a major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment.
    96	- **Bullet points to address:**
    97	  1. May be discussed for people living with type 2 diabetes, given the hormonal changes that can affect blood sugar control; this does not establish eligibility.
    98	  2. May be relevant for patients with significant acid reflux, where a gastric sleeve might worsen symptoms for some people; this does not establish eligibility.
    99	  3. The patient must be ready to commit to lifelong dietary changes and daily vitamin and mineral supplements after surgery.
   100	  4. Eligibility is always a clinical decision made with the patient — BMI is a starting point, not the whole picture. Overall health, history of weight-loss attempts and personal goals all matter.
   101	
   102	---
   103	
   104	### How it works (6 steps)
   105	Each step gets a short heading and 1–2 sentences of content direction.
   106	
   107	1. **GP referral** — The reader asks their GP for a referral to the practice. A referral may allow a Medicare rebate for an eligible consultation, subject to Medicare rules, and is the first formal step toward seeing a surgeon.
   108	2. **Orientation consultation** — The reader meets their surgeon to discuss health, goals and whether a bypass is the right fit. No pressure to decide on the day. Frame this as an open conversation, not a sales meeting.
   109	3. **Pre-operative assessment** — The patient sees dietitians and physicians to prepare for surgery and plan nutrition. A pre-surgery eating plan may be prescribed to reduce liver size; its purpose, duration and risks are explained individually. This is team-led support, not a handout.
   110	4. **Surgery** — Performed under general anaesthetic using a keyhole approach where appropriate, at St John of God Murdoch or Subiaco (hospital identity to be verified — open question). Keep language clinical and calm; timing varies by patient.
   111	5. **Hospital recovery** — The care team manages comfort, starts the patient on fluids, and monitors movement and recovery. Length of stay and recovery timing vary with clinical needs and are confirmed individually.
   112	6. **Ongoing support** — Regular follow-up with surgeon and dietitian well beyond the operation. Position this as a long-term relationship, not a transaction. If weight loss stalls, the team reviews and adjusts rather than leaving the patient on their own.
   113	
   114	---
   115	
   116	### Benefits (5 bullets)
   117	Each bullet conveys one clear, qualified benefit. No superlatives.
   118	
   119	1. **Brief-only stat to retain only after verification:** Clinical averages around 70–80% of excess weight, though individual results vary and no outcome is guaranteed. **Verify: requires a cited clinical source + practice sign-off before publication.**
   120	2. Type 2 diabetes may improve for some patients — some may reduce or stop medication after discussion with their doctor; outcomes vary between individuals.
   121	3. Acid reflux and heartburn may improve for some patients, but outcomes vary and no result is guaranteed.
   122	4. Some patients may see changes in conditions linked to excess weight, such as high blood pressure, sleep apnoea and joint pain; outcomes vary.
   123	5. Long-term hormonal changes may affect hunger and fullness for some patients; eating patterns and outcomes vary.
   124	
   125	---
   126	
   127	### Risks / honest section
   128	- **Intro paragraph tone:** Acknowledge directly that the bypass is a major operation and no surgery is without risk. Give the patient the full picture before deciding, including serious complications that can occur, and explain that the surgeon will discuss how each risk applies individually.
   129	- **Risk bullets to address:**
   130	  1. General surgical risks — bleeding, infection, blood clots, reaction to anaesthetic.
   131	  2. Risk of a leak at one of the new surgical connections — monitored closely during the hospital stay.
   132	  3. Lifelong need for vitamin and mineral supplements, because the rerouting reduces nutrient absorption.
   133	  4. Dumping syndrome — feeling unwell after eating sugary or fatty foods; symptoms may change as eating habits adjust. Your dietitian will explain what to watch for and when to seek advice.
   134	
   135	---
   136	
   137	### Cost paragraph
   138	Cover these points in a single flowing paragraph (not a list):
   139	- Total cost depends on private health cover, surgeon and anaesthetist fees, and length of hospital stay — varies by individual.
   140	- Rather than quoting a single figure that may not apply, the practice provides a clear, itemised written estimate before any decision is made.
   141	- Medicare or private health cover may contribute in some circumstances, subject to MBS criteria, policy terms and waiting periods; readers should confirm this with the relevant providers.
   142	- Some patients may explore early release of superannuation to help with out-of-pocket costs, subject to the applicable rules and individual circumstances.
   143	- Frame this as transparency, not a price pitch.
   144	- Do not quote a specific dollar amount.
   145	
   146	---
   147	
   148	### Conversion CTA headline
   149	Direction: Low-pressure, conversational, direct. Frame it as an offer to talk the decision through — not a push to book. Something in the spirit of: "Ready to find out if a gastric bypass is right for you? Let's talk it through."
   150	
   151	---
   152	
   153	### FAQs (13 questions)
   154	The page should answer all of the following. Writer to draft each answer in plain language, 3–5 sentences, AHPRA-compliant.
   155	
   156	1. How much weight will I lose after a gastric bypass?
   157	2. Will I get loose skin?
   158	3. How long will I need off work?
   159	4. What if it doesn't work for me?
   160	5. Can I eat normally after a gastric bypass?
   161	6. Is gastric bypass better than a gastric sleeve?
   162	7. Do I need a GP referral?
   163	8. Where is the surgery performed?
   164	9. What is the 30/30 rule after gastric bypass?
   165	10. Can gastric bypass be covered by Medicare?
   166	11. How much does gastric bypass cost in Perth?
   167	12. Who is not eligible for gastric bypass?
   168	13. What are gastric bypass outcomes after 10 years?
   169	
   170	---
   171	
   172	## Tone and compliance notes
   173	- **Hospitals (OPEN):** hospital identity is unverified — briefs and drafts name different facilities. Treat every hospital name in public copy as [hospital identity to be verified] until the client confirms.
   174	- **Medical advertising:** No guarantees. Use phrases like "clinical averages", "results vary", "individual results depend on", "outcomes vary between individuals", "your surgeon will advise", "after discussion with their doctor".
   175	- **AHPRA/TGA:** No testimonials, no before/after claims, no guaranteed outcomes. The diabetes claim ("some patients reduce medication") must be qualified with "after discussion with their doctor" and "outcomes vary between individuals".
   176	- **Voice:** Plain-language, empathetic, direct. Slightly more clinical than the sleeve page is appropriate given the procedure's complexity — but still written for a nervous patient doing research, not for a medical journal.
   177	- **Reader:** A Perth adult, likely with type 2 diabetes or significant reflux, who has been researching weight loss surgery and is now comparing procedures. They want to know whether bypass is right for them specifically, and they are sceptical of hype.
   178	- **Surgeon credentials to include:** Dr Harsha Chandraratna (MBBS, FRACS, Bariatric Surgeon; personal bariatric surgery experience; lecturer at University of Notre Dame) and Dr Stephen Watson (MBBS, FRACS, Bariatric Surgeon; keyhole bypass and other weight-loss procedures at SJOG Murdoch and Subiaco). Both operate at St John of God Murdoch and Subiaco (hospital identity to be verified — open question).
   179	- **Clinic locations to mention:** Consultations at Booragoon (Garden City Specialist Centre) and Mandurah (WA Cardiology Centre).

