145-## Tone and compliance notes
146:- **Hospitals (OPEN):** hospital identity is unverified (drafts name different facilities). Treat every hospital name as [to be verified] pending client confirmation.
147-- Medical advertising: no guarantees, use phrases like "clinical averages", "results vary", "your surgeon will advise"
148-- AHPRA/TGA: no testimonials, no before/after claims, no guaranteed outcomes
149-- **AHPRA s133 (eligibility-specific):** State all eligibility criteria as GENERAL INDICATORS subject to individual surgeon assessment — never "if your BMI is X you qualify" or any self-qualifying language. Do NOT pair eligibility with guaranteed outcomes or specific weight-loss / comorbidity-resolution figures (e.g. no "resolve your diabetes", no "lose X kg"). Reference non-surgical options and multidisciplinary (MDT) assessment as part of the pathway — do not push surgery as the default. Include a clear "general information, not medical advice — individual assessment required" line on the page.
     1	# Content Brief: Am I Eligible for Weight Loss Surgery?
     2	**URL:** /am-i-eligible
     3	**Primary keyword:** am i eligible for weight loss surgery
     4	**Search intent:** informational
     5	**Target word count:** 1,000–1,300 words (matches brief target; sufficient to beat thin Australian clinic candidate pages which run 600–1,100w, while staying focused for an informational intent that does not reward long comparison-style depth)
     6	
     7	> **Repositioned per client (June 2026):** medical eligibility, moved out of the finance cluster. "Am I eligible?" is a CLINICAL question (BMI, comorbidities, prior attempts, individual assessment), not a financial/affordability one — frame it medically throughout. Keep internal links cost → eligibility → book-consultation intact.
     8	
     9	## Page purpose
    10	This page answers the single most common question a research-stage patient asks before calling. It removes the fear of being "turned away" before they even pick up the phone, and moves them toward booking an orientation consultation.
    11	
    12	## Keyword Intelligence
    13	
    14	### Primary Keyword
    15	- Keyword: am i eligible for weight loss surgery
    16	- AU Monthly Volume: 10
    17	- Keyword Difficulty: 0/100
    18	- CPC (AU): ~$1.4
    19	
    20	> Note: the related phrase "weight loss surgery eligibility" has ~0 measurable AU search volume, and the head term itself is tiny (10/mo). This is primarily an ACTIVATION/CONVERSION page for anxious, research-stage patients who are already on-site — not an organic-traffic play. Optimise for reassurance and funnel progression (toward booking the consultation), not for raw search volume.
    21	
    22	### Secondary Keywords (target naturally in body copy)
    23	| Keyword | AU Vol/mo | KD |
    24	|---|---|---|
    25	| gastric sleeve surgery eligibility | 20 | 0 |
    26	| gastric sleeve perth eligibility | 10 | 0 |
    27	| weight loss surgery requirements | 10 | 0 |
    28	| do i qualify for weight loss surgery quiz | 10 | 0 |
    29	| weight loss surgery medicare | 20 | 0 |
    30	| weight loss surgery perth | 250 | 45 |
    31	| gastric sleeve perth | 150 | 7 |
    32	| weight loss surgery cost | 350 | 0 |
    33	
    34	### Semantic Terms to Include
    35	These are LSI/semantic terms the writer must naturally weave into the copy. They are not keyword-stuff targets — they are terms that signal topical depth to Google:
    36	
    37	bariatric surgery, gastric sleeve, sleeve gastrectomy, gastric bypass, BMI (body mass index), class 2 obesity, class 3 obesity, comorbidities, type 2 diabetes, obstructive sleep apnoea, high blood pressure, PCOS, fatty liver disease (NAFLD), FRACS surgeon, GP referral, Medicare rebate, private health insurance, superannuation early release, dietitian, psychological assessment, orientation consultation, St John of God Murdoch, Subiaco, metabolic surgery
    38	
    39	### SERP Landscape (AU)
    40	SERP is dominated by two content types. (1) High-authority informational reference pages — Healthdirect (#1, DR 87), Mayo Clinic (#6, DR 92), plus Cleveland Clinic, NIDDK and NHS deeper down — these are generic "is it an option for you / requirements" guides that capture most of the visible traffic. (2) A large long tail of bariatric clinic 'Am I a Candidate / Am I Eligible' pages (obesity.com.au, Keyhole Surgery Centre, O Clinic Sydney, Mercy Bariatrics, Brisbane Obesity Clinic, plus Perth players Perth Surgical Bariatrics, Perth Obesity Solutions, Evolve Bariatrics) — almost all low DR (0–31) and pulling negligible traffic. Google also surfaces a People Also Ask cluster (minimum weight, how to get approved, do I qualify in Australia, who is NOT eligible). Note this is a very low-volume head term (10/mo AU, KD effectively 0), so ranking is achievable but the real value is intent quality and feeding the conversion funnel, not raw volume.
    41	
    42	Top 5 ranking pages:
    43	| # | Domain | Page Title |
    44	|---|---|---|
    45	| 1 | healthdirect.gov.au | Guide to bariatric surgery - Healthdirect |
    46	| 3 | obesity.com.au | Am I a Candidate for Weight Loss Surgery Melbourne VIC |
    47	| 4 | keyholesurgerycentre.com.au | Am I a Candidate \| Weight Loss Surgery |
    48	| 5 | oclinic.sydney | Eligibility for Weight Loss Surgery Sydney |
    49	| 6 | mayoclinic.org | Weight-loss surgery: Is it an option for you? |
    50	
    51	### Content Gap Opportunity
    52	Clear opportunity for a Perth-specific, reassurance-led eligibility page. Gaps: (1) The clinic pages that rank are generic BMI tick-box checklists; almost none address the emotional barrier (shame, fear of being turned away, failed diets) — the brief's empathetic angle is genuinely differentiated. (2) No top result clearly explains Australian Medicare/MBS rebates, private health cover, or superannuation early release in plain language tied to eligibility — own the 'GP referral vs self-referral' and funding angle. (3) PAA shows strong demand for 'who is NOT eligible' and 'minimum weight / BMI around 30' — directly answer the low-BMI (30–34 with comorbidity) and mental-health questions the competitors gloss over. (4) Almost no AU competitor names a specific Perth hospital/surgeon credential (SJOG Murdoch/Subiaco, FRACS) — local trust signals are a fast win. (5) Healthdirect/Mayo win on authority but are non-local and offer no booking path — capture the bottom-funnel 'now what do I do in Perth' intent they cannot serve. Match the PAA questions in FAQ schema to compete for those featured-snippet slots.
    53	
    54	### Recommended Word Count
    55	1,000–1,300 words (matches brief target; sufficient to beat thin Australian clinic candidate pages which run 600–1,100w, while staying focused for an informational intent that does not reward long comparison-style depth)
    56	
    57	## Section guidance
    58	
    59	### Hero
    60	- Headline direction: Echo the reader's own question back to them — direct and non-clinical. It should feel like the page heard them.
    61	- Subheadline: 2–3 sentences that acknowledge the emotional reality (years of trying, not a willpower problem), signal that this page will give them real answers, and make explicit there is no obligation or pressure in getting an answer.
    62	
    63	### Stats bar (3 stats)
    64	Use these verbatim — they are factual anchors the writer must not alter:
    65	
    66	| Label | Value | Note |
    67	|---|---|---|
    68	| MBS threshold | Assessed individually | BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment. |
    69	| First consult | 30–60 min | An honest conversation about your options, with no obligation |
    70	| Consult to surgery | 3–6 months | A typical timeline, though it varies for each person |
    71	
    72	### What is eligibility?
    73	- Section title: "Eligibility is about more than a single number"
    74	- ~3 paragraphs
    75	- Para 1: Explain that while BMI is part of the picture, eligibility is a whole-person assessment — weight, health conditions, history of prior attempts, and goals. Avoid making it sound like a tick-box exercise.
    76	- Para 2: Give a plain-language explanation of the BMI scale (overweight 25–29.9, class 1 obesity 30–34.9, class 2 obesity 35–39.9, class 3 obesity 40+). Make clear that where someone sits influences the options, not a final ruling.
    77	- Para 3: Give a concrete example of two people with the same BMI getting different recommendations — one with type 2 diabetes and sleep apnoea versus one with no comorbidities. This shows why in-person assessment matters.
    78	- Closing note (below paragraphs): Reassure the reader they do not need to calculate their BMI precisely before calling — the team can help, and the surgeon will confirm it at the consult.
    79	
    80	### Eligibility section
    81	- **Frame this as CLINICAL eligibility, not financial.** The section centres on medical indicators: BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment; previous attempts at non-surgical weight loss, age and general health considerations also form part of assessment. Cost, finance and affordability are NOT eligibility criteria — keep them out of this section (they belong in the Cost paragraph and the Medicare/finance page).
    82	- **Present criteria as GENERAL INDICATORS, never a self-qualifying test.** Frame every threshold as a guide that is subject to individual surgeon assessment — never "if your BMI is X you qualify". Make clear surgery is one option within a broader pathway that may include non-surgical options and multidisciplinary (MDT) assessment; do not present surgery as the default outcome of being "eligible".
    83	- BMI summary line to anchor the section: "BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment. A BMI below 35 does not meet the MBS threshold for these items; your surgeon will assess your full health picture and discuss any other clinical pathways."
    84	- Write each of the following as a short, plain-language paragraph or bullet — do not combine them:
    85	  - BMI 27–29: not usually the first step; the medical team can discuss non-surgical medical weight management and dietitian support
    86	  - BMI 30–34 with a weight-related condition: does not meet the MBS threshold for these items; the surgeon may discuss other clinical pathways after individual assessment
    87	  - BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment
    88	  - BMI 40 or above: MBS eligibility is assessed individually against the applicable item, clinical and referral requirements; BMI alone does not guarantee eligibility
    89	  - Comorbidities relevant to assessment: a major obesity-related comorbidity matters to MBS assessment at BMI 35–39.9, but a condition does not create eligibility below BMI 35. Whether a particular condition counts as a major obesity-related comorbidity is assessed individually against the current MBS criteria
    90	  - History of failed diets: this may inform assessment but does not establish eligibility by itself; the surgeon considers the full clinical picture
    91	  - Mental health: part of the assessment. A mental health history does not automatically rule out surgery, but active or unstable concerns, an eating disorder, substance misuse or difficulty following aftercare may affect timing or suitability
    92	
    93	### How it works (4 steps)
    94	Keep steps in this order and cover this content — 1–2 sentences each:
    95	
    96	1. **Get in touch — self-referral or GP** — both routes work; explain that a GP referral may allow a Medicare rebate for an eligible specialist consultation, subject to current requirements, but is not required to get started
    97	2. **Orientation consultation** — 30–60 minutes, relaxed and honest; surgeon explains suitable options, walks through procedures, discusses costs openly; no obligation to proceed
    98	3. **Assessment and preparation** — if the patient decides to move forward: tests, meeting with a dietitian and (where helpful) psychologist; this stage helps the surgeon assess whether surgery is appropriate, explain the risks and prepare the patient for what comes next
    99	4. **Surgery and aftercare** — procedure scheduled once everything is in place; Perth-based aftercare team provides follow-up during recovery and the months that follow; long-term experiences vary between individuals and ongoing follow-up remains part of care
   100	
   101	### Benefits (5 bullets)
   102	Each bullet should convey one of these ideas — write them as patient-facing outcomes, not clinical claims:
   103	
   104	1. A clear, personalised answer about whether surgery is right for them — ends the guessing
   105	2. Alternative pathways may be discussed if surgery is not the right starting point (non-surgical medical weight management, dietitian support) — do not imply that every reader is suitable for a particular pathway
   106	3. For many patients, surgery supports meaningful improvement in weight-related conditions like type 2 diabetes and sleep apnoea — but individual results vary, use measured language
   107	4. Access to FRACS-certified surgeons at SJOG Murdoch and Subiaco (hospital identity to be verified — open question: drafts name different facilities)
   108	5. A team that takes prior attempts seriously and builds a plan around the individual — not a one-size-fits-all template
   109	
   110	### Risks — honest section
   111	- Intro paragraph: Set a tone of respect for the reader's intelligence. Acknowledge that being eligible does not automatically make surgery the right choice; part of the assessment is making sure the patient understands both the commitment and the risks so they can decide clearly.
   112	- Risk bullets to address (one sentence each, plain language):
   113	  - All surgery carries risk: bleeding, infection, blood clots, anaesthetic reactions
   114	  - Bariatric procedures require lifelong changes to eating habits and ongoing follow-up
   115	  - Some procedures require long-term vitamin and mineral supplementation
   116	  - Outcomes vary between individuals and are influenced by many factors, including post-surgery dietary and lifestyle changes and ongoing follow-up
   117	  - Surgery is a treatment option, not a cure; the care team will explain its intended role, limits and ongoing requirements
   118	
   119	### Cost paragraph
   120	Cover: costs and any Medicare or private-health benefits depend on the procedure, policy and individual circumstances; discuss them at the first consultation and ask Medicare and the insurer to confirm what applies. The team provides a clear itemised estimate. Do not quote specific dollar figures or promise that a particular policy will cover a component.
   121	
   122	### Conversion CTA headline
   123	Direction: Acknowledge the emotional weight of the first step (making contact feels hard). The line should be warm and direct — it should make the reader feel that picking up the phone is safe, not a commitment.
   124	Reference tone: "The hardest part is picking up the phone. Let us help you find out where you stand."
   125	
   126	### FAQs (11 questions)
   127	The page must answer each of the following:
   128	
   129	1. My BMI is around 30 — am I too low for weight loss surgery?
   130	2. I have tried every diet and they all failed. Does that count against me?
   131	3. Will a mental health history stop me from being eligible?
   132	4. Do I need a GP referral, or can I refer myself?
   133	5. What actually happens at the first appointment?
   134	6. How long does it take to go from first consultation to surgery?
   135	7. What if I find out surgery is not right for me?
   136	8. How much does weight loss surgery cost in Perth?
   137	9. Does Medicare cover weight loss surgery?
   138	10. What is the 30/30 eating rule after bariatric surgery?
   139	11. What can happen over 10 years after weight loss surgery?
   140	
   141	(Paste-ready answers for Q8–Q11 are in the content review; keep dollar figures out of copy and treat the 30/30 protocol as to-be-confirmed with the practice.)
   142	
   143	---
   144	
   145	## Tone and compliance notes
   146	- **Hospitals (OPEN):** hospital identity is unverified (drafts name different facilities). Treat every hospital name as [to be verified] pending client confirmation.
   147	- Medical advertising: no guarantees, use phrases like "clinical averages", "results vary", "your surgeon will advise"
   148	- AHPRA/TGA: no testimonials, no before/after claims, no guaranteed outcomes
   149	- **AHPRA s133 (eligibility-specific):** State all eligibility criteria as GENERAL INDICATORS subject to individual surgeon assessment — never "if your BMI is X you qualify" or any self-qualifying language. Do NOT pair eligibility with guaranteed outcomes or specific weight-loss / comorbidity-resolution figures (e.g. no "resolve your diabetes", no "lose X kg"). Reference non-surgical options and multidisciplinary (MDT) assessment as part of the pathway — do not push surgery as the default. Include a clear "general information, not medical advice — individual assessment required" line on the page.
   150	- **GLP-1 / medical-weight-management policy:** TGA/legal review remains pending. Prefer the neutral phrase "non-surgical medical weight management" in public copy; if an active ingredient or medication class is retained after the decision, use only semaglutide, liraglutide or GLP-1 wording, never brand names, and flag the copy for verification.
   151	- Voice: plain-language, empathetic, direct; avoid hype
   152	- Reader: a Perth adult considering weight loss surgery; anxious, doing research, probably has tried multiple diets, possibly carrying shame — write to the person, not the condition
   153	- The page should lower the emotional barrier to calling; every section should reduce fear, not add clinical weight
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:180:> **3. Will a mental health history stop me from being eligible?** A mental health history does not automatically rule out surgery. Psychological wellbeing is part of the assessment, and active or unstable concerns, an untreated eating disorder, substance misuse or difficulty following aftercare may affect timing or suitability. Your surgeon will discuss what support or further review may be needed.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:213:> **4. Do I need a GP referral, or can I refer myself?** You can contact the practice directly; a GP referral is not required to get started. A referral may allow a Medicare rebate for an eligible specialist consultation, subject to current requirements, but the practice and Medicare should confirm what applies to your appointment and fees.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:273:> **1. My BMI is around 30; am I too low for weight loss surgery?** A BMI of 30-34 does not meet the MBS threshold for these items. Your surgeon can assess your wider clinical picture and discuss non-surgical medical weight management or dietitian-led care where appropriate; an individual assessment is still required.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:327:> **7. What if I find out surgery is not right for me?** Some people are not suitable for surgery straight away, or may not be suitable at all — suitability depends on an individual clinical-risk and aftercare assessment that your surgeon will explain. If surgery is not the right fit, non-surgical options can be discussed where clinically appropriate.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:329:> **8. How much does weight loss surgery cost in Perth?** Costs vary by procedure, hospital, surgeon, anaesthetist, tests, private-health cover, excess, waiting periods and individual circumstances. Any applicable range or estimate is discussed at consultation, with an itemised breakdown before you decide.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:331:> **9. Does Medicare cover weight loss surgery?** Medicare benefits may apply when the relevant MBS item, clinical and referral requirements are met, but Medicare does not cover every cost and no rebate should be assumed. The applicable item depends on the procedure—31575 is sleeve gastrectomy, 31572 is Roux-en-Y gastric bypass and 31581 is BPD/duodenal switch—so your surgeon and insurer should confirm what applies.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:333:> **10. What is the 30/30 eating rule after bariatric surgery?** The 30/30 rule is an eating habit often recommended after bariatric surgery: take around 30 minutes to eat each meal, and avoid drinking fluids for 30 minutes either side of eating. Your dietitian will confirm the exact protocol the practice recommends for you. [30/30 protocol to be confirmed with the practice before publication]
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:335:> **11. What can happen over 10 years after weight loss surgery?** Long-term experiences vary. Some people maintain changes, while others regain weight, develop nutritional issues or need further treatment; ongoing medical, dietary and supplement follow-up may remain important. Your surgeon can discuss the evidence and what your health and follow-up plan may mean for you, but no single 10-year outcome can be promised.
