1723:+   > The 30/30 rule is an eating habit often recommended after bariatric surgery: take around 30 minutes to eat each meal, and avoid drinking fluids for 30 minutes either side of eating. Your dietitian will confirm the exact protocol the practice recommends for you. [30/30 protocol to be confirmed with the practice before publication]
1724-+   >
1725-+   > **10. Who may not meet the Medicare pathway for weight loss surgery?**
1726-+   > The MBS thresholds are BMI 40+, or BMI 35–39.9 with a major co-morbidity. A person below BMI 35 does not meet that MBS threshold; a person at BMI 35-39.9 needs the relevant major co-morbidity. Clinical suitability also depends on medical history, surgical risk, readiness for follow-up and individual clinician assessment.
1727-+   >
1728-+   > **11. Does Medicare cover weight loss surgery in Australia?**
1729-+   > Medicare may provide rebates for eligible specialist consultations and MBS-listed bariatric surgery services when current criteria are met. The relevant procedure items are 31575 for sleeve gastrectomy, 31572 for Roux-en-Y gastric bypass and 31581 for BPD/duodenal switch. Private health cover, waiting periods, hospital excess and anaesthetist fees can affect out-of-pocket costs, so confirm what applies before deciding.
1730-+   >
1731-+   > **12. What are the 10-year outcomes after weight loss surgery?**
1732-+   > Ten-year outcomes vary by procedure, health history, follow-up and other factors. Weight regain or changes in related conditions can occur, so no permanent result should be promised. Ask your surgeon to explain the long-term evidence, trade-offs and follow-up relevant to the procedure being considered.
