63	> **7. Does Medicare cover gastric sleeve surgery?** Medicare item number 31575 attracts a rebate when eligibility criteria are met, offsetting part of the surgical fee. However, Medicare alone doesn't cover the full cost and hospital, theatre, and anaesthetist fees still apply. Private health insurance with a gold-tier (or equivalent) policy will cover the hospital component. A full fee breakdown is available to help you understand your out-of-pocket costs.
67	Why it matters: The cost-section replacement covers the earlier insurance claim; this later FAQ repeats the absolute claim. Coverage depends on fund rules, policy settings, waiting periods and gap arrangements, so the copy should not imply automatic cover.
73	> It is typically performed ‘laparoscopically’ via keyhole surgery, which tends to mean an easier recovery than some more invasive procedures.
77	> Significant, sustained weight loss; an estimated clinical average of 60–70% of excess weight over 12–18 months, though individual results vary.
83	> Reduced appetite; the procedure reduces stomach size and lowers ghrelin (‘hunger hormone’) production, so smaller portions feel more satisfying.
87	> Keyhole approach; means less scarring, generally less post-operative pain, and a shorter hospital stay (and recovery time) than open surgery.
91	> Gastric sleeve surgery is well-established and generally safe, but it is still a major surgery which carries with it some real risks, all of which your surgeon will discuss with you in detail.
95	> Staple line leak; though it is a serious potential complication and may require further treatment, it only occurs in ~1–3% of cases.
99	> Reflux or heartburn can occur or (if pre-existing) worsen after a sleeve. In most people it settles, though for others it can be persistent.
103	> Hair thinning in the months after surgery is typically linked to rapid weight loss and reduced nutritional intake; it’s usually temporary and improves as your intake stabilises.
107	> **4. How is a gastric sleeve different from a gastric bypass?** A gastric sleeve reduces stomach size but leaves the digestive pathway unchanged. A gastric bypass both reduces the stomach *and* reroutes the small intestine, which can cause some nutrient malabsorption. The bypass may produce slightly greater weight loss and tends to suit patients with significant reflux better.
