   228	> **Compress the loose-skin FAQ answer to two sentences. Source: extracted draft L259-L266.**
   229	>
   230	> Loose skin is common after significant weight loss. How much loose skin your weight loss results in depends on your age, your genetics, your skin elasticity, and of course, how much weight you lose. Exercise, particularly resistance training, can help with body composition during your weight loss phase. You might look into plastic surgery for loose skin (‘body contouring’ and ‘skin removal’) as an option down the track, though bear in mind it is not covered by the same funding pathways.
   231	>
   232	> Loose skin can occur after significant weight loss, and the amount varies with factors such as age, genetics, skin elasticity and the amount of weight lost. Some patients discuss body-contouring or skin-removal surgery after their weight has stabilised; funding and eligibility differ from bariatric surgery, so confirm any coverage with your surgeon and insurer.
   233	
